176:
The divine splendor, which is the result of the meritorious deeds of my friend (Visākhā) born in the Nimmānarati heaven, is inconceivable and immeasurable. 'Ananyathā' means without deviation, according to reality (as she answered). How did this goddess know of Visākhā's splendor? Just as Subhaddā knew Bhaddā, the goddess Visākhā had also come to this goddess before.

Now, the goddess, wishing to urge the Elder to encourage others in giving, taught the Dhamma with these verses:

“Therefore, encourage others as well (in merit);
Give gifts to the Saṅgha with a joyful heart;
Listen to the Dhamma with a confident mind;
The rare gain of human birth has been obtained.

The Lord of the Path, whose voice is sublime and whose skin is like pure gold, has taught which path;
In which (recipients) gifts given bear great fruit;
Give gifts to the Saṅgha with a joyful heart.

The eight individuals praised by the virtuous constitute these four pairs;
They are the disciples of the Sugata, the Saṅgha, worthy of offerings;
Gifts given to them bear great fruit.

Four practicing for the Path and four established in the Fruit;
This is the upright Saṅgha, endowed with wisdom, virtue, (and concentration).

For humans seeking merit, living beings performing merit that leads to rebirth (upadhi);
A gift given to the Saṅgha bears great fruit.

For this Saṅgha is extensive and has reached greatness; it is immeasurable like the great ocean;
These noble disciples of the Hero of Men, the light-makers, proclaim the Dhamma.”
